<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azes I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 58–35 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Azes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azes I was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> king of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 1st century BCE who ruled over Gandhara and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. He is widely thought to have founded the Azes era, which began in about 57 BCE and was used in later inscriptions, though its exact start is debated. His abundant coins continued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> bilingual designs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azes-i.jpg" alt="Azes I" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uploadalt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Azes_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
